{{/* Your own account (av-qo05, epic av-g2dx). Data: profilePageData
     (profile.go).

     A person acting on themselves. It wears /admin/users' furniture — the
     settings shell below, settings.css, the settingsHeader partial — and
     shares none of its authority: that page passes adminOnly because it
     reaches other accounts, this one reads only ownerIDFromCtx's.

     Laid out as sections from the first one, though only Account has content
     today. The BYO agent key, active sessions and export are the rest of
     av-g2dx, and each should land as a new card rather than a rearrangement.

     Login names, email addresses and provider subjects are all text this
     instance did not author, and they reach the markup as template values
     where html/template's contextual escaping keeps them text. */}}
{{define "profile"}}<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Your account — Exhibit</title>
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{.Favicon}}">
{{template "pwaHead"}}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/phosphor/regular.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/gallery/tokens.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/gallery/components.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/gallery/settings.css">
</head>
<body>
{{template "settingsHeader" "Your account"}}
<main class="settings">
<div class="lede">
  <h2>Your account.</h2>
  <p>What this instance knows about you, and what you can do about it. Everything here acts on your account alone — never anyone else&#39;s.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3 class="settings-h3">Account</h3>
  {{if .Account.SignedIn}}
  <dl class="settings-facts">
    <dt>Signed in as</dt>
    {{/* An identity provider is not obliged to send an email, and users.email
         is NOT NULL DEFAULT '' rather than guaranteed — so the name can be
         the provider's subject claim instead, and when it is, the page says
         so. An opaque identifier offered as "you" reads as a bug; the same
         identifier labelled as what it is reads as an answer. */}}
    <dd>{{if .Account.Name}}<span class="settings-name">{{.Account.Name}}</span>{{else}}<span class="muted">Signed in through your identity provider</span>{{end}}</dd>
    <dt>Sign-in</dt>
    <dd>{{if .Account.HasPassword}}<i class="ph ph-key" aria-hidden="true"></i> Password{{else}}<i class="ph ph-shield-check" aria-hidden="true"></i> Identity provider{{end}}</dd>
  </dl>
  {{if .Account.NameIsSubject}}<p class="muted text-sm settings-note">Your identity provider sent no email address for this account, so the name above is the subject identifier it does send. It is the string an administrator would use to find you.</p>{{end}}
  {{if not .Account.HasPassword}}<p class="muted text-sm settings-note">Your identity provider owns this sign-in. There is no password here to change, and Exhibit cannot change one there.</p>{{end}}
  {{else}}
  {{/* No login is configured on this instance, so sessionGate is a
       pass-through, ownerMiddleware supplied the single-user default, and
       there is no users row behind it. Nothing is missing — that is the
       self-hoster's default shape — so the page says what it is instead of
       showing an account it made up. */}}
  <p class="muted">This instance has no login configured, so there is no account to describe. Its  belongs to whoever can reach the origin, and the API is guarded by the service token alone.</p>
  {{end}}
</div>

{{/* The danger zone (section: av-qo05; the deletion behind it: av-4wyq).

     The copy is the hard part and it is load-bearing. Deleting here erases
     what Exhibit holds and cannot touch the identity provider that issued the
     login — and because users.external_id is UNIQUE and the row is created
     just-in-time at login, the same person signing in again lands in a new,
     empty account. The confirmation says that outright: someone who deletes,
     finds their login still works and concludes nothing happened is worse off
     than someone who never had the button at all.

     Two steps rather than one. The first states what goes, including how many
     share links other people hold that stop working; the second asks for a
     typed phrase. A click can be a mis-tap, and there is no undo anywhere near
     this — no soft delete, no trash, no snapshot (av-1rvm covers state
     durability separately).

     Both steps render server-side, revealed by profile.js rather than built by
     it. These are the most important sentences on the page, and a component
     with exactly one definition, in a template, is this project's rule for
     precisely that reason. */}}
<div class="card card-danger">
  <h3 class="settings-h3 settings-h3-danger"><i class="ph ph-warning-octagon" aria-hidden="true"></i> Delete account</h3>
  <p class="settings-danger-copy">Deleting your account erases everything you've created, including shared artifacts. Every artifact and its file, and all other account data. It is permanent. There is no undo, no trash, and no copy kept on this side.</p>
  {{if .Account.SignedIn}}
  {{/* Counted rather than gestured at, and shares first among equals: an
       artifact is your own, but a share link is a URL somebody else may be
       holding, with no account here and no way to be told it stopped
       working. */}}
  <p class="settings-danger-copy">This account holds <strong>{{.Account.Artifacts}}</strong>.{{if .Account.ShareCount}} Deleting it breaks <strong>{{.Account.Shares}}</strong> that other people may be holding.{{end}}</p>
  {{/* What deletion reaches stops at this instance, and which sentence says so
       depends on where the sign-in lives. Telling someone with a password here
       that their identity provider is unaffected would name a provider they do
       not have. */}}
  {{if .Account.HasPassword}}
  {{else}}
  <p class="settings-danger-copy">Next time you sign in with your identity provider, you will be treated as a new user.</p>
  {{end}}
  {{end}}
  <div class="settings-foot">
    {{if .Account.DeleteBlocked}}
    <button class="btn btn-danger" type="button" id="delete-account" disabled aria-describedby="delete-account-reason">
      <i class="ph ph-trash" aria-hidden="true"></i> Delete account
    </button>
    <span class="muted text-sm" id="delete-account-reason">{{.Account.DeleteBlocked}}</span>
    {{else}}
    <button class="btn btn-danger" type="button" id="delete-account" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="delete-confirm">
      <i class="ph ph-trash" aria-hidden="true"></i> Delete account
    </button>
    {{end}}
  </div>

  {{if not .Account.DeleteBlocked}}
  <div class="settings-confirm" id="delete-confirm" hidden>
    {{/* Assembled from clauses that are each conditional on their own count,
         because a sentence built around "your no artifacts" is how a
         confirmation stops being read. */}}
    <p class="settings-danger-copy"><strong>This cannot be undone.</strong> Everything created or owned by you is permanently inaccessible{{if .Account.ArtifactCount}} including {{.Account.Artifacts}} and everything saved inside{{end}}.{{if .Account.ShareCount}} {{.Account.Shares}} will stop working for whoever is holding them.{{end}}</p>
    {{if .Account.HasPassword}}
    <p class="settings-danger-copy">If you revisit this site, you will be treated as a new user.</p>
    {{else}}
    <p class="settings-danger-copy">Signing in again will still work via your identity provider. You will arrive at a new account, with an empty gallery.</p>
    {{end}}
    <label class="settings-label" for="delete-phrase">Type <code>delete my gallery</code> to confirm</label>
    <input class="field settings-confirm-field" type="text" id="delete-phrase" autocomplete="off"
           autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false" placeholder="delete my gallery">
    <div class="settings-foot">
      <button class="btn btn-danger" type="button" id="delete-confirm-go" disabled>
        <i class="ph ph-trash" aria-hidden="true"></i> Delete my gallery permanently
      </button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" id="delete-cancel">Cancel</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  {{end}}
  {{/* One live region for the outcome, the same idiom /admin/users uses. The
       refusals worth reading in full are the ones this page can still get
       after the button was enabled — the last-admin race, above all — so it is
       a block of text rather than a toast that leaves. */}}
  <div class="settings-status" id="profile-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
</div>
</main>

<script>
const TOKEN = {{.Token}};
{{/* The same per-request credential contract every other page carries
     (av-5imk, pagecredential.go): a session-authenticated browser is handed
     no token, because its cookie already authenticates it. The deletion above
     is a mutation through the JSON API like any other client's, so it spends
     this rather than inventing a credential of its own. */ -}}
const READ_ONLY = {{if .ReadOnly}}true{{else}}false{{end}};
</script>
<script src="/assets/gallery/api.js"></script>
<script src="/assets/gallery/profile.js"></script>
</body>
</html>{{end}}
